nd `tasks`, following the spec's state machine, naming and logging rules.\n- **`datasette/app.py`**: `add_background_task()`, `start_background_tasks()` and `_launch_background_tasks()`. `_suppress_background_tasks` and the supervisor are set up in `__init__`. Both `AsgiLifespan` and `AsgiRunOnFirstRequest` now launch tasks after the startup sequence.\n- **`datasette/cli.py`**: `serve --get` sets `_suppress_background_tasks = True` before building the `TestClient`, so startup hooks still register tasks but nothing launches.\n- **`datasette/__init__.py`**: re-exports `BackgroundTask` and `BackgroundTaskSupervisor`.\n\n**Deviations from the spec**\n- The spec says `background_tasks.py` is standard-library only, but it needs `pm` to find the owning plugin. Importing `datasette.plugins` at module level caused a circular import through `datasette/publish/heroku.py`. I moved that import inside `_plugin_name_for`, which is the only place it is used. The module now imports nothing from datasette at load time.\n- When several plugins match a function's module, the spec doesn't say which to pick. I take the first match from pluggy's listing and did not special-case the most specific one.\n\n**Other notes**\n- `background_tasks.py` changed on disk during the session. The content I re-read matches my own edits, so I left it alone.\n- I did not run ruff/black or the full test suite; only the four spec test files were run.", "ttft_ms": 2532, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 49269, "uuid": "35018b95-555d-4abd-a52a-fdc4c3cdd5a9", "ttft_stream_ms": 2335, "time_to_request_ms": 401, "first_content_frame_ms": 2335, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
